027 #edb/ref 17592186049762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428635 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Green's Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1033 2116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1034 1.061673902190143 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051070 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7e860cf4-4488-5596-ab86-e913faeb242e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186536640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177819 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "215217ab-57d4-55ca-a3b0-79e58a5c7e34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179524 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179524 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179524 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179527 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179527 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179527 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179600 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179600 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179600 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1039 "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179738 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179857 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "912b55f4-8877-5aff-bc43-d73698914e66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f08d86dc-4f87-54db-ae1a-88ae0082bc5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180025 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=z,Q=x,R=y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180173 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180175 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/10/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9aa3be4f-70c9-57d5-8b81-21b143de9479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180279 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9fd222d255fd1a37f00cbe7c9abf6af0d0e3af92f9ac581591bb1062780e69fa.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ABCA,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70ad4034-9f56-5cca-8517-cdacbbf852a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180280 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180386 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180386 #edb/ref 1061 23622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180386 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7ff2ff0-c95d-5155-89fb-bfd7eb079783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180386 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180387 #edb/ref 1061 23623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180387 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5214fa4f-2dd0-5f35-827a-aa639d600260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180387 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1061 23747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6acb0f4b-5b56-5e4c-a728-4db2fd840bff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180390 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1061 35726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7465601-d18a-5a90-bfe9-a27fe52c871f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1061 35769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4246c091-bba6-587e-8d43-72e5c01a1e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180501 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1064 "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1065 3976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d745eed3-7e69-55af-b685-1c7482dfffb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180678 #edb/ref 1067 "Reversing the Orientation of a Curve and Sums Over Unions of Paths" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1064 "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1065 5837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b4ca8cae-81df-5c55-90ac-839b063e78a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180722 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257003 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19e81440-e84d-56cd-bb58-ec161c442251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257614 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{C}(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}x-4\\,\\text{d}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257614 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257614 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5eb6d40c-1738-5868-a121-ed6bf6c0d399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257618 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257618 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257618 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f494e8c-ad73-595a-bf25-8c0366227915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257809 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+(3y-1)\\,\\text{d}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257809 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re