\\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fe/fec5c3e96eeed9fb01479c0127e2d530a628855a124d8ab1ef380c48c2f569f1.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $LMNL,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a005f1b5-54c0-59d7-8a93-206dab6e0652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345277 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
